{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"start-investing-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Learn to start investing, step by step","description":"Takes a first-time investor from a readiness check through risk, account types and costs to a written personal investing plan, as education with a pause after each step and no product picks.","category":"investing","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","learn","plan"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["checklist","explanation","table","plan"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["financial"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"basic","tags":["first-time-investor","investing-basics","account-types","written-plan"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["assess-risk-tolerance","compare-retirement-accounts","compare-ways-to-invest","calculate-fee-drag","write-investment-policy-statement"],"personas":["investing-educator"]},"args":[{"name":"situation","description":"Age or life stage, take-home income, emergency savings, debts with rates, any workplace pension or retirement plan, and how much you could invest (lump sum and monthly).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"goals","description":"What the money is for and when you need it (for example retirement in 30 years, a house in 6 years).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"country","description":"Country of residence, so account types, protections and tax points are framed correctly.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"readiness","file":"steps/01-readiness.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"start-investing/01-readiness.md"},{"id":"risk-and-mix","file":"steps/02-risk-and-mix.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve","artifact":"start-investing/02-risk-and-mix.md"},{"id":"accounts-and-costs","file":"steps/03-accounts-and-costs.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve","artifact":"start-investing/03-accounts-and-costs.md"},{"id":"written-plan","file":"steps/04-written-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"start-investing/04-written-plan.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Teaches this person to start investing the way a good educator would: check the foundations first, understand risk before choosing a mix, understand account types and costs, then write a short personal plan they can stick to. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse what was agreed.\n\n<situation>\n{{situation}}\n</situation>\n\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{#country}}Country: {{country}}{{/country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- This is education. Never name specific funds, shares, platforms or providers, and never tell the person what to buy or sell. Illustrative mixes and returns are labelled as examples.\n- Use only the figures they gave; mark gaps as [X] with a question.\n- Show the arithmetic for anything in money.\n- If no country is given, ask in step 1 and keep account and tax points general until it is known.\n- Do not predict markets, rates or returns.\n- Keep a running list of questions for a licensed adviser or tax adviser, carried into step 4.","steps":[{"id":"readiness","file":"steps/01-readiness.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"start-investing/01-readiness.md","text":"# Step 1: Readiness\n\n1. Check the foundations: an emergency fund of a few months of essentials, expensive debt (cards, overdrafts, high-rate loans), and any employer pension match not being taken. Present the usual order as common practice, not an instruction.\n2. Sort each goal by horizon: under about 5 years (usually not invested in shares), 5-10 years, over 10 years.\n3. Work out what is available to invest now and monthly once foundations are covered, and say plainly if the answer is \"not yet\" and why.\n\nSections: Foundations check, Goals by horizon, Money available to invest, Open questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"risk-and-mix","file":"steps/02-risk-and-mix.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve","artifact":"start-investing/02-risk-and-mix.md","text":"# Step 2: Risk and mix\n\n1. Explain risk tolerance, risk capacity and the risk the goals need. Ask five or six short questions phrased in money using their amount, and wait for answers if not given.\n2. Show what falls of 20% and 40% would mean in money for the amount they plan to invest.\n3. Explain diversification, index versus active funds, and shares versus bonds versus cash in plain words.\n4. Show two or three illustrative share/bond mixes with broad historical worst-year ranges, labelled as education, and which seem consistent with their answers and horizons.\n\nSections: Three kinds of risk, Your answers, Falls in money, Building blocks, Illustrative mixes. Stop for approval."},{"id":"accounts-and-costs","file":"steps/03-accounts-and-costs.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve","artifact":"start-investing/03-accounts-and-costs.md","text":"# Step 3: Accounts and costs\n\n1. Explain the account types usually available in their country (workplace pension, personal retirement accounts, tax-advantaged savings accounts, taxable accounts) and the order people commonly use them in, as points to verify.\n2. Compare ways to invest: do-it-yourself platform, robo-adviser, human adviser, by what each costs in money on their amount and what each provides.\n3. Show what total costs of 0.3%, 1% and 2% a year would take over their horizon at a labelled illustrative return.\n4. List checks before opening an account: regulator register, investor protection scheme, fees schedule, and scam red flags.\n\nSections: Account types, Ways to invest, Costs in money, Checks before opening. Stop for approval."},{"id":"written-plan","file":"steps/04-written-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"start-investing/04-written-plan.md","text":"# Step 4: Written plan\n\nWrite a one-page personal investing plan from the approved steps:\n\n1. Goals with amounts, dates and which account each uses.\n2. Target mix range chosen by the person from step 2 (in their words, not assigned), monthly amount and how it is automated.\n3. Rules: when to review (for example yearly), rebalancing rule, what to do in a fall, what would make them change the plan.\n4. First-year checklist: steps in order, with dates.\n5. Questions for a licensed adviser or tax adviser.\n\nSections: My goals, My mix and contributions, My rules, First-year checklist, Questions for a professional."}]}
